# input-assertion-no-mutable-input.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M134a): an input-handling assertion on
# a request that has nothing to mutate — demonstrates `tflw check`'s TF067 (checker, tflw M134a /
# D382, the pentest arc's Tier 3).
#
# `expect response has no input handling violations` re-sends the request the last `api` step
# actually made, once per (mutable input × payload), with exactly one input replaced each time. That
# needs an input. `api GET /health` has no identifier path segment, no query parameter and no JSON
# body — so no probe can be planned, no rule can apply, and the assertion cannot fail. An assertion
# that cannot fail reports a green line over an empty test, which is the one outcome a scan must
# never produce: D285's rule, arriving here under its own code because the repair is specific.
#
# **The keyword is two bare words.** `input handling`, with a space, never `input-handling`. tflw's
# `isIdentCont` is `/[A-Za-z0-9_]/` and `-` lexes as `minus`, so a hyphen cannot appear in a keyword
# at all — the language has no hyphenated bare keyword anywhere, and every multi-word construct in it
# is space-separated. The plan for this tier specified the hyphenated spelling and it could not ship;
# writing it here would be a parse error long before TF067 was reached.
#
# **The checker answers `false` on every uncertainty, and that is what the last test in this file is
# for.** A `{var}` in the path may bind to something id-shaped; a `body from` file is not the
# checker's to read; a raw text body may well be JSON. Each of those *might* offer a site, so the
# checker stays silent and the interpreter decides at run time with the real request in hand. This
# rule refuses a correct file in exactly zero cases, which is the direction a checker may not be
# wrong in — and a fixture that only demonstrated the firing half would let the silent half widen
# without anything going red.
#
# **The absences matter as much as the rule, and they are why this fixture looks unlike its Tier 2
# neighbours.** Tier 2's scan replaces the identity, so it needs an owner (TF063) and refuses a step
# carrying its own credential (TF062). Tier 3 changes **no** identity — only the payload moves — so
# neither code has an analogue here: an unowned test is fine, and a step naming its own
# `Authorization` header is fine. Nothing below declares `as <session>`, and that is deliberate
# rather than an omission. If a future change makes an owner necessary, this file goes red.
#
# **Not covered here:** the run-time twin of this code. The checker catches the literal cases; the
# interpreter catches every case, including the interpolated ones above, and fails the assertion with
# the same repair. That door needs a live server and belongs to the acceptance corpus, not to a
# check-only fixture. Also not covered: `probe oversized` / `probe traversal`, which are *config*
# dialect and are demonstrated where the other `authorized target` sub-clauses are.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw's header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/input-assertion-no-mutable-input.tflw

test "an input-handling assertion with nothing to mutate"
  api GET /health
  expect response has no input handling violations

test "a severity floor does not supply the missing input"
  api GET /health
  # The floor narrows which rules run. It has nothing to do with whether there was anything to send.
  check response has no input handling violations

test "a form body provably yields no JSON leaf, so it is the same defect"
  api POST /orders form a=1, b=2
  expect response has no input handling violations

test "the sites the rule must not touch"
  # One of each kind: an identifier path segment, a query parameter, a JSON body leaf. Each of these
  # is a real mutation site, so TF067 stays silent — this is the negative control that stops the rule
  # widening into the case the feature exists for.
  api GET /orders/7
  expect response has no input handling violations

  api GET /products?q=shoes
  expect response has no input handling violations

  api POST /orders body { note: "hi" }
  expect response has no input handling violations

test "every uncertainty answers maybe, and stays silent"
  # Interpolation can produce an id segment or a whole query string; a file-backed body is not the
  # checker's to read; raw text may well be JSON. None of these is decidable here, so none reports.
  let id = "7"
  api GET /orders/{id}
  expect response has no input handling violations

  api POST /orders body text "{a:1}"
  expect response has no input handling violations
